# Applied to every Maven invocation run from backend/ — CI and local alike.
#
# Without this, any build against a not-yet-populated local repository dies
# before a single goal executes:
#
#   java.lang.ClassCastException: org.apache.http.impl.client.BasicAuthCache
#   cannot be cast to org.apache.http.client.AuthCache
#     at org.eclipse.aether.transport.http.HttpTransporter.<init>
#
# Two different ClassRealms: HttpTransporter is loaded from a realm that
# carries its own httpclient, and the AuthCache interface it casts to comes
# from Maven's core realm. spring-cloud-contract-maven-plugin is declared with
# <extensions>true</extensions>, so its realm is built during project graph
# construction — which is exactly where the stack trace lands. Selecting the
# wagon transport avoids the native HTTP transporter entirely.
#
# Only reproducible with an empty local repository, because a warm ~/.m2 means
# the resolver never has to open a transport at all. That is why it passed on
# developer machines for so long and failed on the first CI run. Verified by
# running `./mvnw -Dmaven.repo.local=<fresh dir> -DskipTests package` with and
# without this flag: without it, the ClassCastException; with it, BUILD
# SUCCESS, contract stubs and stubs jar generated normally.
#
# The real fix is to stop the plugin needing an extension realm — it has no
# <executions>, so removing <extensions>true</extensions> means declaring
# convert/generateTests/generateStubs by hand. Worth doing; not worth blocking
# a red build on.
-Dmaven.resolver.transport=wagon
